{{History of Balisca}}

The '''Mutiny of the Basilio''' ({{Langx|bl|Motín Basilio}}) was a pivotal naval [[W:Mutiny|mutiny]] that occurred off the coast of [[Bayara]] in early September 1827. Led primarily by [[Afro-Baliscans|Afro-Bayaran]] sailors, the mutiny of the Bayaran frigate ''[[BB Basilio|Basilio]]'' directly challenged the escalating policies of [[Maroon removal|forced displacement]] in Bayara and served as a critical catalyst for the convening of the [[1827 Confederal Convention]], and Bayara's moves [[Xunta Nacionalista|towards secession]].

Like many ships in the confederal navy forged during the War of Independence, the ''Basilio''<nowiki/>'s crew was notably diverse; it included a significant proportion of [[Mestizo Baliscans|Mestizo]] and [[Mozarabia|Afro-Mozarab]] sailors, many of whom were [[W:Free persons of color|free persons of color]], descendants of [[Maroon pirates|Maroon corsairs]], or possessed direct family ties to the communities targeted by removal policies. Their presence on Bayaran ships reflected their wartime service (some of whom were [[W:Impressment|impressed]]) and the complex, often fragile, integration of diverse peoples into the nascent post-independence order.

